WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
Close your eyes. Where is your elbow?

2
00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.060
You know without looking.

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:09.880
But how?

4
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:14.230
Inside limb muscles lie stretch sensors called muscle spindles.

5
00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.950
They sit alongside the ordinary fibres.

6
00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.670
Stretch the muscle, and the spindle stretches too.

7
00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.210
Its sensory endings fire faster.

8
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.330
The primary ending, called type Ia, is especially sensitive to small, rapid

9
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:30.330
stretches.

10
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.980
A vibrator on a tendon delivers exactly that.

11
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.560
Many tiny stretches, every second.

12
00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:42.783
Goodwin, McCloskey and Matthews held a vibrator on the biceps tendon, at 100 Hz.

13
00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:44.923
The elbow did not move.

14
00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:48.097
The biceps runs in front of the elbow.

15
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:50.797
It gets longer as the arm straightens.

16
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:57.950
Predict. If the brain reads the biceps spindles' firing as biceps stretch, which way

17
00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.310
should the elbow seem to move?

18
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.640
Toward a straighter arm.

19
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.560
Subjects judged the elbow to be where it would be if the vibrated muscle had been

20
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:12.560
stretched.

21
00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:16.913
Vibrate the triceps, behind the elbow, and the arm seemed more bent.

22
00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:21.933
During contraction, the error could reach 40 degrees in that experiment.

23
00:01:22.853 --> 00:01:24.133
It is not a standard size.

24
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:30.027
A spindle's firing carries no label of its cause.

25
00:01:30.927 --> 00:01:33.887
With the arm still and unseen, the brain read it as stretch.

26
00:01:34.917 --> 00:01:40.197
The one idea: limb position is an estimate, built mainly from spindle signals of

27
00:01:40.197 --> 00:01:41.197
muscle length.

28
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:45.747
Skin, joint and motor-command signals are added to that estimate.

29
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.520
So in a still, unseen arm, vibrating one tendon usually biases the estimate toward

30
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.520
that muscle being stretched.

31
00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.120
A typical direction, not a guaranteed response.

32
00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.580
And when proprioceptive input is severely impaired, accurate movement depends

33
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:03.580
heavily on vision.

34
00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:09.933
In touch and pain, capsaicin opened the heat channel TRPV1 on pain fibres.

35
00:02:10.873 --> 00:02:12.233
It felt hot, with no heat.

36
00:02:13.213 --> 00:02:16.613
Here, a vibrator drives spindle endings while the joint stays still.

37
00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:21.677
One caveat. Primary endings are the likely carriers.

38
00:02:22.617 --> 00:02:27.437
In a small leg-muscle sample, they tended to respond more to low-amplitude vibration

39
00:02:27.437 --> 00:02:29.177
than secondary endings.

40
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.910
But in contracting muscle, secondary endings and tendon organs respond too.

41
00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.270
So primary endings are not shown to act alone.

42
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.780
So vibrating one tendon can make a still limb feel moved.

43
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.040
The brain reads the extra spindle firing as stretch.
